abstract['en']=f'We evaluate hard-budget feature repair in EHOA with fold-local mutual-information utility and selected-column imputation, scaling and SMOTE. The five-seed primary analysis comprises 750 runs on ten datasets, using 15 dependent final masks per method and dataset. A prospectively published extension adds a complete endpoint 2x2x2 budget/R/lambda design across ten datasets (240 runs) and a paired 16-hiker, 20-iteration search study (150 runs). Including the earlier biomedical sensitivity study, 1464 fold-safe runs are available. Steel uses 27 predictors and seven classes; Parkinson recordings use subject-prefix-disjoint inner and outer folds. Resampled repair attains balanced accuracy {mean.loc["stable_lcb","balanced_accuracy"]:.4f}, cost ratio {mean.loc["stable_lcb","cost_ratio"]:.4f} and Nogueira stability {mean.loc["stable_lcb","nogueira_stability"]:.4f}. Friedman balanced-accuracy p is {f.loc["balanced_accuracy","p_raw"]:.5f}. Corrected paired comparisons qualify the conclusions. All constrained outputs respect their budgets. The public repository retains code, public data attribution, protocol commits and audited results. Synthetic costs, unresolved clinical subject identity and lack of independent external clinical data prevent clinical or monetary claims. The extension was designed with earlier outcomes known and is not retrospective preregistration.'
